From 7695849c414c04fd6b237a6a50325c43402ddfbf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Schachter <davidschachter@appdevforall.org>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:01:52 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] Return ArrayBuffers and typed arrays as BLOBs

A BLOB argument reaches JavaScript as an ArrayBuffer, but a function
that returned an ArrayBuffer, or a typed array such as Uint8Array, gave
NULL: js_value_to_sqlite() turned every object into NULL. So no function
could return binary data, and `identity(x'00ff')` with
`(function(args){ return args[0]; })` returned NULL. db.exec() bound an
ArrayBuffer but not a typed array, and bound an empty ArrayBuffer as
NULL.

Add js_get_bytes(), which gets the bytes of an ArrayBuffer or of the part
of a buffer that a typed array views, and use it for function results and
for db.exec() parameters. An empty buffer is an empty BLOB. Returning a
detached buffer is an error; binding one still binds NULL, as before.
A typed array's offset and length come from its byteOffset and
byteLength getters, read before its bytes: one that tracks the length
of a resizable buffer keeps the length QuickJS recorded when it was
made, which goes stale when the buffer is resized, and reading it
after a shrink would read past the buffer. A typed array of fixed
length that its shrunken buffer no longer holds is an error.
Document how values convert in both directions in the README.

Add a Blobs test: BLOBs round-trip through a function, typed arrays,
subarrays and ArrayBuffers made in JavaScript become BLOBs, a function
can build a BLOB from a BLOB, a detached buffer is an error, and
db.exec() binds typed arrays and empty buffers as BLOBs. It also checks
typed arrays over resizable buffers that shrink and grow.

